# conn_ctl: the control channel of a connection console (bin/console started
# with the argument "conn", user/services/console/conn.c; docs/M13-PLAN.md,
# "The terminal layer"). A connection console is a terminal whose screen and
# keyboard are a connection: program output goes out as bytes on its master
# channel and the bytes typed come in on it (hand-written, <termconn.h>); the
# size of the remote terminal's window comes on this channel, so the
# programs on the console (console.size) see it change.
#
# Who serves it: the console. Who calls it: the process that made the
# console's channels and holds their other ends (init's session service, a
# library's pseudo-terminal), with the remote terminal's size when it
# changes. The console takes it on its startup role CONN_CTL_ROLE; without
# one the size stays what its arguments said.
#
# Restart: a connection console keeps nothing across a restart. Its owner
# made the master and control channels for one console, so when the console
# ends (the master hung up, or it crashed) both of the owner's ends see
# ERR_PEER_CLOSED and the owner ends the session, or starts a console again
# with new channels; nothing is asked again.

protocol conn_ctl 42

# The remote terminal is now cols x rows cells. The console answers
# console.size with it from now on (a grid wider than 480 or taller than 180
# is reported as 480 x 180: the window console's own limits, so every client's
# buffers sized for them hold). 0 for either: ERR_INVALID_ARGS.
1  resize  (u16 cols, u16 rows) -> ()
